WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.367
Welcome to Module Nine, Lesson Three: privacy, audit, and governance.

2
00:00:04.697 --> 00:00:08.637
You're tested on the engineering side of the rules, not legal detail.

3
00:00:09.437 --> 00:00:14.101
Picture BrightLoan, an online lender whose agent pre-screens loan applications.

4
00:00:14.431 --> 00:00:16.216
Then three hard questions arrive.

5
00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:19.193
An applicant wants their data erased,

6
00:00:19.243 --> 00:00:22.683
and a regulator asks why a March application was declined.

7
00:00:23.013 --> 00:00:26.057
An auditor asks if the agent is fair to every group.

8
00:00:26.857 --> 00:00:31.180
Privacy starts with minimisation: send only the fields the model needs.

9
00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:35.876
Redact or tokenise personal details before they reach a model or a log.

10
00:00:36.676 --> 00:00:39.847
If data can't leave, self-host the model with NIM.

11
00:00:40.177 --> 00:00:43.263
And limit how long prompts, traces, and memory are kept.

12
00:00:44.063 --> 00:00:46.552
An erasure request must reach every copy.

13
00:00:46.882 --> 00:00:51.355
The vector index, memory, traces, and caches, not just the main database.

14
00:00:52.155 --> 00:00:55.945
The regulator needs an audit trail, which is not application logging.

15
00:00:56.275 --> 00:01:00.705
It's immutable, tamper-evident, access-controlled, and retained per policy.

16
00:01:01.505 --> 00:01:04.052
It records the input, who asked, the sources,

17
00:01:04.102 --> 00:01:06.937
and every tool call with its arguments and result.

18
00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:11.591
Plus approvals, the output, and the exact model, prompt, and policy versions.

19
00:01:12.391 --> 00:01:15.946
With that record, BrightLoan can reconstruct the March decision.

20
00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:18.103
That's the answer the regulator wants.

21
00:01:18.903 --> 00:01:23.760
Credit is high-risk under the EU AI Act, which tiers obligations by risk.

22
00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.968
High-risk uses need human oversight, logging,

23
00:01:27.018 --> 00:01:30.547
transparency, documentation, and conformity assessment.

24
00:01:31.347 --> 00:01:34.761
NIST's AI Risk Management Framework has four functions:

25
00:01:34.811 --> 00:01:36.587
govern, map, measure, manage.

26
00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:44.163
ISO/IEC 42001 is certifiable, and model cards document intended use and limits.

27
00:01:44.963 --> 00:01:48.668
Then fairness: the agent is ninety-two percent accurate overall.

28
00:01:48.998 --> 00:01:51.551
Split by subgroup, one group fares far worse.

29
00:01:52.351 --> 00:01:56.802
Bias enters through data, retrieval, prompts, and majority-only test sets.

30
00:01:57.132 --> 00:01:59.489
So evaluate by subgroup, audit sources,

31
00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:02.821
red-team with varied inputs, and offer an appeal path.

32
00:02:03.621 --> 00:02:04.413
On the exam,

33
00:02:04.463 --> 00:02:10.312
regulated data plus an outside model API means redact at the boundary, or self-host.

34
00:02:10.642 --> 00:02:14.027
A contract alone isn't an engineering control, so read on.
